// Documented open front-end/link defect (book-verification 2026-09-19, repros r_print /
// r_print2). Spec §5.1.1 defines `print(value: string) -> atom proc[device_io]` as a built-in
// intrinsic string-printing function alongside `println` -- the same section, same signature
// shape, same "Intrinsic Functions" status as every other print_* builtin (§7417: "not defined
// in user code or standard libraries but are built-in language primitives"). The compiler
// implements `print_string` (see string_addition/newline.silica and every other trial in this
// suite) but never registers a builtin literally named `print`, so a call to `print(...)`
// silently falls through the front end as an ordinary unresolved user call: it type-checks and
// compiles (the effect checker does special-case the identifier "print" for its device_io
// effect, so no compile-time diagnostic fires), then fails only at LINK time with
// "Undefined symbols ... main_print" because no user function named `print` exists either.
// Expected: compiles, links, runs, and behaves exactly like print_string -- prints "yes" then a
// newline, exit code 2 (module atom table index of :ok, as in this suite's other trials).
//   yes
//   2
// Observed: link failure ("Undefined symbols for architecture arm64: \"main_print\", referenced
// from: main in main.o ... ld: symbol(s) not found").

fn main() -> atom {
    sequence proc[device_io]
        _: atom <- print("yes");
        println("")
    produces
        pure :ok
    end
}
